        'html' => '<p>Many small businesses don\'t lose customers over price or quality. They lose them between "someone got in touch" and "someone got back to them": a message read and forgotten, a missed call, a website form buried under twenty other emails.</p>
<p>Better lead follow-up closes that gap, and it doesn\'t need an expensive system. Here are five steps, from the simplest to the more advanced.</p>
<h2 id="part-1">Step 1: Know where your inquiries come from</h2>
<p>Before fixing anything, get the full picture. Write down every way a customer can reach you: phone, WhatsApp or text, your website form, Facebook and Instagram messages, email, maybe a booking site.</p>
<p>Most small businesses find three to five channels, each checked in a different place. That\'s usually where leads fall through.</p>
<h2 id="part-2">Step 2: Put everything in one place</h2>
<p>Every inquiry, from every channel, should land in the same place: a CRM, a shared spreadsheet, or at least one list. The tool matters less than having just one.</p>
<p>This part is easy to automate. A website form can add a row to your sheet on its own, a new message can be logged automatically, and a lead from an ad can be tagged with its source. Nobody has to remember to copy anything.</p>
<h2 id="part-3">Step 3: Reply fast, even when you\'re busy</h2>
<p>The person who just got in touch is the warmest lead you\'ll ever have. A few hours later, they\'ve probably contacted two other businesses too. That\'s the idea behind speed to lead: the sooner the first reply, the better your chances.</p>
<p>You can\'t always reply yourself straight away, but you can make sure the customer hears back straight away:</p>
<ul><li>An automatic confirmation that says when you\'ll get back to them.</li><li>A <a href="{BASE}en/articles/whatsapp-chatbot">WhatsApp chatbot</a> that answers common questions and collects details.</li><li>An automatic email to anyone who fills in your website form.</li></ul>
<h2 id="part-4">Step 4: Make lead follow-up routine</h2>
<p>Most deals don\'t close on the first contact. A customer asked for a quote and went quiet? That doesn\'t mean they aren\'t interested. Sometimes they simply forgot.</p>
<p>Decide once what happens after each stage:</p>
<ul><li>Quote sent → a follow-up reminder two or three days later.</li><li>Appointment booked → a reminder the day before.</li><li>No reply → one more message a week later.</li><li>Job done → a review request a few days after.</li></ul>
<p>You set these up once and they run on their own. That\'s exactly what <a href="{BASE}en/articles/small-business-automations">small business automation</a> is for.</p>
<h2 id="part-5">Step 5: Measure, once a month</h2>
<p>Once a month, check how many inquiries came in and from which channel, how many you answered and how quickly, and how many became customers. It takes fifteen minutes.</p>
<p>Once you see the numbers, the leak is obvious. Maybe WhatsApp works well and the website form doesn\'t. Maybe you reply quickly but never follow up. Each answer points to the next fix.</p>
