        'html' => '<p>Say "small business automation" and many owners picture a large, expensive software project. In practice, the automations that save the most time are small: one task you repeat dozens of times a week, now happening on its own.</p>
<p>A useful rule of thumb: <strong>anything someone does by hand, the same way, over and over, can usually run without them.</strong></p>
<p>Here are five workflows almost any small business can start with. You don\'t need all of them.</p>
<h2 id="part-1">1. A first reply to every inquiry</h2>
<p>A customer gets in touch through your website, WhatsApp or an ad. Instead of waiting until you\'re free, they get an instant message: "Thanks, we\'ve got your request and will get back to you today by 6 p.m." It can also ask the questions you\'ll need answered anyway.</p>
<p><strong>What it saves:</strong> customers who go elsewhere because nobody replied. If most of your inquiries arrive on WhatsApp, a <a href="{BASE}en/articles/whatsapp-chatbot">WhatsApp chatbot</a> takes this a step further.</p>
<h2 id="part-2">2. Appointment reminders</h2>
<p>An automatic message the day before an appointment, with the time, the address and a way to confirm or cancel. People who cancel early leave you time to fill the slot instead of sitting through an empty hour.</p>
<p><strong>What it saves:</strong> no-shows, and the "just reminding you about tomorrow" calls.</p>
<h2 id="part-3">3. Email automation</h2>
<p>A few sequences that work for you in the background:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Welcome</strong> emails for new customers or subscribers, with what they need to know.</li><li><strong>Quote follow-up</strong> when a quote goes unanswered.</li><li><strong>Abandoned cart</strong> emails for online stores: a gentle reminder for someone who started checking out and didn\'t finish.</li><li><strong>Review requests</strong> a few days after the job.</li></ul>
<p><strong>What it saves:</strong> writing the same emails again and again, and sales lost because nobody followed up.</p>
<h2 id="part-4">4. Connecting your tools</h2>
<p>A lead arrives from your website → it\'s added to your sheet or CRM → tagged by source → you get a notification. No copy and paste, and nobody slips through.</p>
<p>The same idea works the other way: a new order creates a task, a payment updates the customer\'s status, a booked appointment appears in your calendar. Tools like Zapier or Make, or the built-in integrations of your CRM and booking system, handle most of this. CRM integration doesn\'t require an expensive system either; a well-organized spreadsheet is enough to start. How to organize the leads themselves is covered in the <a href="{BASE}en/articles/stop-losing-leads">guide to lead follow-up</a>.</p>
<p><strong>What it saves:</strong> entering the same information in three places, and the mistakes that come with it.</p>
<h2 id="part-5">5. A weekly report that sends itself</h2>
<p>Every Monday morning, an email arrives with the numbers that matter: how many inquiries came in, from which channel, and how many turned into customers. No logging in to five different tools.</p>
<p><strong>What it saves:</strong> the feeling of not knowing what\'s happening in your business, and the time spent pulling numbers together.</p>
<h2 id="part-6">Where to start with small business automation</h2>
<p>You don\'t need to set everything up at once. Ask yourself two questions:</p>
<ol><li><strong>What takes up most of my time each week?</strong></li><li><strong>Where do I lose the most customers?</strong></li></ol>
