    [
        'slug' => 'conversion-tracking',
        'title' => 'Conversion Tracking: How to See What Your Ads Actually Bring In',
        'meta_title' => 'Conversion Tracking: How to See What Your Ads Actually Bring In',
        'description' => 'Paying for Google and Meta ads without knowing what works? What conversion tracking is, how to set it up, and how to stop paying for ads that don\'t sell.',
        'keyword' => 'conversion tracking',
        'type' => 'Problem & solution',
        'service' => 'services#campaigns',
        'topic' => 'campaigns',
        'published' => '2026-11-28',
        'updated' => '2026-11-28',
        'image' => 'assets/img/articles/en/conversion-tracking.webp',
        'image_alt' => 'Conversion tracking report: inquiries by source, Google, Instagram, Facebook and organic',
        'minutes' => 3,
        'html' => '<p>"I spend money on ads every month and I have no idea what\'s working." If that sounds familiar, you\'re not alone. Your ad dashboards show clicks, impressions and likes, but the question that matters stays unanswered: <strong>how many customers did this bring in, and what did each one cost?</strong></p>
<p>The answer starts with one thing: <strong>conversion tracking.</strong></p>
<h2 id="part-1">What is conversion tracking?</h2>
<p>A conversion is the action that actually matters to your business: a form submitted, a WhatsApp or phone tap, a call, a purchase. Conversion tracking counts each of those actions and records which campaign, ad and keyword it came from.</p>
<p>Without it, Google and Meta can only tell you how many people clicked. With it, you see how many of them became inquiries, and your real cost per lead.</p>
<h2 id="part-2">Why conversion tracking matters</h2>
<ol><li><strong>You know where to put your money.</strong> If campaign A brings in leads cheaply and campaign B brings in nothing, the decision makes itself.</li><li><strong>The ad platforms learn.</strong> When Google and Meta know what a conversion looks like, their bidding looks for more people like the ones who converted. Without tracking, they optimize for people who click, which isn\'t the same thing.</li><li><strong>You stop guessing.</strong> Budget, ad and audience decisions are made on numbers instead of gut feeling.</li></ol>
<h2 id="part-3">What to measure</h2>
<p>Not every action is a conversion. Focus on what leads directly to a customer:</p>
<ul><li><strong>A form submission</strong> on your website or landing page.</li><li><strong>A tap on WhatsApp or your phone number.</strong></li><li><strong>A purchase</strong> in an online store, including its value.</li><li><strong>A booked appointment,</strong> if you use a booking system.</li></ul>
<h2 id="part-4">How to set up conversion tracking properly</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Install the tags:</strong> the Google tag for Google Ads conversion tracking, and the Meta Pixel for Facebook and Instagram ads.</li><li><strong>Define events:</strong> each action above is sent as its own event, with a clear name.</li><li><strong>Connect them to your campaigns:</strong> mark in Google Ads and in Meta Events Manager which events count as conversions.</li><li><strong>Add server-side signals:</strong> Meta\'s Conversions API sends events from your server or CRM, and Google\'s enhanced conversions sends hashed first-party data such as an email address. Both help count conversions that browser tags miss.</li><li><strong>Test that it works:</strong> submit the form yourself and confirm the conversion shows up. This is the step most often skipped, and it\'s exactly where bad data comes from.</li><li><strong>Connect it to your CRM (advanced):</strong> then you see not only which campaign brought in a lead, but which one brought in a customer who actually paid.</li></ol>
<h2 id="part-5">Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Double counting:</strong> the same conversion is recorded twice, so the campaign looks better than it is.</li><li><strong>Measuring the wrong thing:</strong> a page view counted as a conversion instead of a real inquiry.</li><li><strong>Tracking that breaks silently:</strong> a website change removes the tag and nobody notices for months.</li><li><strong>No link to business results:</strong> you know how many leads came in, but not how many became customers.</li></ul>
<h2 id="part-6">What comes after measurement</h2>
<p>Now the real optimization starts: put more budget behind what brings inquiries at a good cost, pause what doesn\'t, and test new ads and audiences against real numbers. At the end of each month you should have a simple report: how much you spent, how many inquiries came in, and what changes next month.</p>
<p>Even the best campaign won\'t help if the <a href="{BASE}en/articles/landing-page">landing page</a> doesn\'t convert, or if <a href="{BASE}en/articles/stop-losing-leads">leads don\'t get a reply in time</a>.</p>
<h2 id="part-7">Sources</h2>
<ul><li><a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/1722022" target="_blank" rel="noopener">About conversion measurement (Google Ads Help)</a></li><li><a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9888656" target="_blank" rel="noopener">About enhanced conversions (Google Ads Help)</a></li><li><a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/meta-pixel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meta Pixel (Meta for Developers)</a></li><li><a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/marketing-api/conversions-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conversions API (Meta for Developers)</a></li></ul>',
